WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:03.040
Hey, everybody, my name is Bradley Knapp, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:07.200
we've come together today to talk&nbsp;
about what is a dedicated host?

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:11.280
Dedicated host is a construct that&nbsp;
you see a lot in the world of cloud,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:16.240
but it's a little confusing. What does this&nbsp;
dedicated host thing actually mean? Why does it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:20.960
look like it's so expensive when you compare it&nbsp;
to buying a dedicated instance or just a normal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:27.600
virtual server? And the answer is dedicated&nbsp;
hosts are a way to provide single tenancy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:33.200
Single tenancy means that no one else can&nbsp;
run on the compute that you are running on.

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:38.720
So, if we think about a physical cloud host,&nbsp;
right, we've got our actual cloud server here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:43.840
This is the actual host that runs in the data&nbsp;
center. In a normal multitenant environment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:48.480
we would take, and we would divide that host up&nbsp;
into pieces, and every one of these pieces as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:54.880
virtual server that you would buy. I'll just mark&nbsp;
that as VSI. In a dedicated host, we still have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.880 --> 00:01:02.080
the same host, that same box that we were talking&nbsp;
about before. But instead, this is your box,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:07.920
and you control all of the VSIs that schedule on&nbsp;
to it. So instead of this being customer A, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:13.680
this being customer B, and this being customer C,&nbsp;
this is all yours. And so you can choose to divide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:19.200
it up however you want. You can have one third of&nbsp;
it go into one VSI and two thirds go into another.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:25.840
You can divide it half and half, or you&nbsp;
can put lots of little bitty ones on it.

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:31.120
And so, the question, of course, always&nbsp;
becomes, why would I do this, what is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:37.600
value of a dedicated host? Why would I purchase&nbsp;
this thing? And the answer is really twofold.

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:42.160
First is that single tenancy that I mentioned&nbsp;
before. Single tenancy means that you don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:46.480
have to worry about anybody else being able&nbsp;
to crash through the hypervisor layer within&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:51.840
this host and get to your data. Even though it's&nbsp;
already incredibly secured in the world of cloud,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:56.160
it's just one more layer of protection. This&nbsp;
is very important for health care workloads,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.160 --> 00:01:58.560
folks in banking and finance like that.

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:03.120
But in addition to that single tenancy,&nbsp;
it also allows you to schedule workloads&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:08.640
that want to be close to be each other, close to&nbsp;
each other. So, if we think of our example here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:14.080
where we've got two VSIs of the same size, let's&nbsp;
imagine a high-performance compute workload,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:20.320
something that in the world of HPC, where you&nbsp;
have lots of compute working on the same problem.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:25.680
In order to minimize latency and maximize&nbsp;
performance, you want to pack these onto a few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:31.280
physical hosts as possible because data transit&nbsp;
through a physical host is much faster than data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:36.013
that goes across the network. If we think about&nbsp;
our example over here, where we've got our multi&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.013 --> 00:02:42.160
tenancy, and we've got multiple multitenant&nbsp;
boxes, every one of these has to link together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:47.680
and that link together takes time. There is a&nbsp;
latency penalty that you pay. But in a dedicated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:54.080
host like we have over on this side, because these&nbsp;
all run on the same physical box, you don't incur&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:58.960
that latency penalty and you can order multiple&nbsp;
dedicated hosts in order to make that happen.

